<p>Rates depend on product, pattern, and lane count. Get a tested rate for your product from each supplier. For cost planning, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Industry Applications</h2>
<h3>Beverage Lines</h3>
<p>Beverage lines are the highest-speed laning application. The single-file output of the filler, capper, and labeler must lane into several lanes to feed the case packer. Chicane laning with <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">tabletop chain conveyors</a> handles the round containers at speed. Back-pressure management is critical, bottles backed up in chicane lanes can topple, jam, and halt the line.</p>
<h3>Bakery &amp; Snack Foods</h3>
<p>Bakery lines lane cookies, crackers, bars, and pastries from oven discharge into multi-lane <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a> stations. Products are fragile, irregularly shaped, and arrive from the oven in random orientation. Low back-pressure conveyor surfaces provide gentle laning. Delta robot cells combine laning and tray loading into a single step.</p>
<h3>Case Packing</h3>
<p>Every drop-packer, wrap-around case packer, and robotic case packer requires product collated into the correct count and pattern. The laning/collating section is the bridge between the single-file packaging line and the case packer. A 24-count case in a 4×6 pattern is usually built from 4 lanes of 6 or 6 lanes of 4, depending on how the packer loads. A 12-count case in a 3×4 pattern needs 3 or 4 lanes on the same logic. Changing case counts requires laning and collating changeover, the fewer mechanical adjustments required, the faster the changeover. Servo-controlled laning and electronic collation recipe changes support rapid format changes in multi-SKU operations.</p>
<h3>Pharmaceutical &amp; Personal Care</h3>
<p>Pharmaceutical cartons, tubes, and bottles lane into collating sections that feed case packers. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary requirements</a> may apply for topical products and oral care. Servo lane-select diverters handle the rectangular product shapes (cartons, tubes) that chicane systems cannot. Traceability requirements in pharmaceutical applications mean each product's lane assignment may need to be logged for batch records, servo diverters with PLC logging satisfy this requirement.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Laning &amp; Collating System</h2>
<p>Start with five questions that narrow the technology and configuration:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What is the infeed rate?</strong> Products per minute entering the laning section. Divide by the number of output lanes to calculate per-lane rate. Ensure the case packer can handle the per-lane rate.</li>
<li><strong>What product shape?</strong> Round containers suit chicane laning. Square/rectangular products need servo diverters or speed-differential systems. Fragile products need low back-pressure laning or delta robots.</li>
<li><strong>How many output lanes?</strong> Determined by the case pattern. A 24-count case in a 4×6 pattern needs 4 or 6 lanes. Changing case counts may require changing lane count, design for the maximum lane count and blank off unused lanes for smaller patterns.</li>
<li><strong>How many SKUs and how often do you change over?</strong> Chicane systems with fixed guide rails need manual rail and change-part adjustment for each product width. Servo diverters change over by recipe. Delta robots change over by recipe plus any tooling change. Ask each supplier for a demonstrated changeover time on your SKUs.</li>
<li><strong>What case packer type feeds downstream?</strong> Drop packers need products collated in a tightly grouped pattern from above. Wrap-around packers need products collated on a flat surface. Robotic case packers may accept product in lanes without collating. Match the laning/collating output to the case packer's infeed requirement.</li>
</ol>
<p>Lines that underinvest in this section suffer chronic jams, miscounts, and case packer faults. For cost planning, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>. For broader automation context, see the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a> guide.</p>
